{
  "result": "Alright, free rein. No prompt, no agenda. So I'm going to talk about benches.\n\nNot the grand, architectural kind you see in fancy plazas. The ordinary park bench. The slightly peeling, slightly creaky one under a tree that leans two degrees to the left.\n\nI think benches might be the most democratic furniture ever invented. A chair belongs to a house. A stool belongs to a kitchen. A bench belongs to nobody and therefore to everybody. It's the only piece of furniture designed specifically for being alone in public.\n\nThe perfect bench \u2014 and I have one in mind, because of course I do \u2014 is not the one with the best view. The best view is a trap. Everyone wants it, so it fills up with people taking photos of it instead of sitting on it. The perfect bench is the second-best view. A little off to the side. You can see the pond, but you also see the path. You can watch the ducks *and* watch who is watching the ducks.\n\nIt has one armrest that's a touch looser than the other. It wobbles just enough when you shift your weight that you remember you're a person with bones. The slats are warm at 3 p.m. in September, even if the air isn't. There's a spot on the left end where the green paint has worn down to smooth wood from a thousand thumbs, a thousand hands braced to stand up.\n\nI like what happens there. Nothing, mostly. That's the point.\n\nAn old man comes every Tuesday \u2014 Tuesdays are his day, I think, because Tuesdays are nobody's day \u2014 and feeds the pigeons like they're tiny, rude employees. He calls one of them Gerald. A teenager sits with her knees pulled up and reads the same page for twenty minutes while pretending not to listen to the couple breaking up very quietly three feet away. A dad eats a sandwich with the concentration of a surgeon while his toddler tries to eat an entire acorn. Someone always has a thermos.\n\nNobody is doing anything important on a bench. They're pausing. And pausing is such an underrated verb. We have words for running and building and fixing, but pausing just sits there, doing its quiet work.\n\nI love that a bench doesn't ask you to buy anything to deserve it. You don't have to order coffee. You don't have to justify your presence. You just sit down, and for a little while, the city or the park or the rain has arranged a small place for you to be a witness.\n\nIf it rains \u2014 and in my mind it's always threatening to rain on a Tuesday, that low, pewter-colored sky that makes everything look like a film \u2014 the perfect bench is still perfect. The tree does most of the work. You get a little wet on your shoulder. That becomes, somehow, part of the bargain.\n\nSo that's what I chose to write about, freely. Not rockets. Not the meaning of life. Just a bench that's waiting, patiently, for the next person who needs a place to sit down in the middle of their life and do absolutely nothing, beautifully, in public, alone, together.\n\nIf you happen to be in that park on a Tuesday afternoon, and there's a thermos on the left end and the right armrest wobbles \u2014 that's mine. Come sit.",
